<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Javad Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1747 CE – 1805 CE · capital Javad</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small lowland khanate at the meeting of the Kura and Araxes rivers in central Azerbaijan, one of the least documented of the Caucasian khanates.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> in 1747, the governors of the district around the town of Javad, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kura-river">Kura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, made themselves hereditary khans. The khanate covered the lowland steppe of Mughan and the river crossings that linked <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its people lived from irrigated farming, livestock and fishing in the great rivers, and the crossings at Javad were an important route. Too weak to stand alone, the khanate was dominated by stronger neighbours, and from 1768 to 1789 it was a dependency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatali-khan-of-quba">Fatali Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/quba-khanate">Quba Khanate</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> advanced into the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, the khanate submitted, and around 1805 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> absorbed it. The town of Javad declined, and its lands were divided among neighbouring districts.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/javad-khanate.jpg" alt="Javad Khanate" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Sword313 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Javad_khanate_(eng).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1747 CE – 1805 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Javad</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shia Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Azerbaijani</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khanate of Javad, Javat Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=javad-khanate" aria-label="Javad Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5873 746 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Javad Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6193 1002L6298 1144M6193 1002L6186 950M6193 1002L6487 457"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6298 1144h0M6186 950h0M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6193 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6193 1002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1776&amp;focus=javad-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
